<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Sherley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1581–<a href="/years/1628">1628</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Sherley was an English adventurer who, with his brother Anthony, travelled to the court of the Safavid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> at the end of the sixteenth century. He later served the shah as an envoy to European courts. The Sherley brothers assisted with Abbas I's military reforms, which created new corps of slave-soldier cavalry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and artillerymen paid by the crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-sherley.jpg" alt="Robert Sherley" width="276" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anthony van Dyck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Dyck_-_Sir_Robert_Shirley,_1622.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Shirley, as the name is also written, was the third son of Thomas Shirley of Wiston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>, and Anne Kempe, daughter of Thomas Kempe of Olantigh in Wye, Kent. His elder brothers were Thomas Shirley and Anthony Shirley, and six of his sisters survived infancy. In 1598 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> with Anthony, who had been sent to Safavid Iran to train its army in the manner of the English militia and to reform its artillery. When Anthony left, Robert stayed on with fourteen other Englishmen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In February 1607 he married Sampsonia, a Circassian Orthodox Christian slave related to Abbas the Great; baptised a Catholic by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a>, she took the name Teresa after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a> and became known in the west as Lady Shirley, Teresa Sampsonia. The modernisation of the Safavid army by Robert and his men is credited with the first decisive Safavid victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, in the Ottoman–Safavid War (1603–1612), which ended on terms highly favourable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p>In 1608 Abbas sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> and other European princes to unite them in an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Sigismund III Vasa received him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>; in Germany Rudolf II, Holy Roman Emperor, made him a count palatine and a Knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and Pope Paul V also made him a count. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with a suite of eighteen on 27 September 1609, then went by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in December 1609, and stayed there with his wife, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, until the summer of 1611.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 1613 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in 1615, when his caravan met the travel writer Thomas Coryate in the desert basins of central Persia. In 1627 he went to Persia a third time with Dodmore Cotton, the first English ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, and died of dysentery at Qazvin soon after arriving. Teresa, who retired to a Carmelite convent attached to Santa Maria della Scala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, had his remains moved there in 1658 and died in 1668.</p><p>The brothers' exploits were dramatised in 1607 in The Travels of the Three English Brothers by John Day, William Rowley and George Wilkins, and Thomas Middleton translated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> panegyric of Robert as Sir Robert Sherley his Entertainment in Cracovia. Van Dyck painted a double portrait of him and his wife, now at Petworth House.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Shirley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Qazvin, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1611" aria-label="England on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4528 338 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Sherley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4978 608h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4978 608h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53,-1&amp;year=1611">What was here in 1611? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
